course: dictionary
lesson: 9485
title: Steam: the mist and the gas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Steam is the hot white mist that rises from boiling water. In science, steam is really water as a gas, and that gas is invisible. «Steam is invisible: the white cloud above a kettle is tiny droplets, and the real steam is in the clear gap just above the spout.» So the word can mean the cloud you see or the gas itself.
explain: Steam can also be a verb. «The soup is steaming» means the soup is giving off steam. To steam food means to cook it over boiling water. The words around steam tell you whether it is a thing or an action.
explain: Context clues help you here too. «Heat milk and a skin forms on top, which traps steam until the milk suddenly rushes over the edge of the pan.» Trapped steam pushes its way out, so you can guess it is a hot gas that needs room to escape. Steam can burn, so keep your face and hands away from kettles and pans.
fact: Steam is water as a gas, made when water is heated to 100 °C.
fact: Steam is a noun (the steam) and also a verb (the soup is steaming).
fact: To let off steam means to release energy or anger, and to run out of steam means to get tired.
fact: «Nobody used steam to do work until the first steam engines around 1700», and those engines powered trains and factories.
fact: When steam touches a cold window, it condenses back into water drops.
exercise: «Steam is invisible: the white cloud above a kettle is tiny droplets.» What is the white cloud above a kettle? | A smoke from a fire | Hot air only | Steam as a gas | *Tiny droplets of water
exercise: Mum says, «Keep your face away from the pan, the steam can burn you.» Why? | *Steam is very hot and can burn | Steam is a solid, like ice | Steam is cold and wet | Steam is only a smell
exercise: In the sentence «The soup is steaming», what kind of word is steaming? | A number | A noun, meaning a hot mist | A place | *A verb, meaning giving off steam
test: What is steam in science? | *Water as a gas, made when water is heated to 100 °C | Hot mist from a kettle | Ice on a window | Metal parts of an engine
test: Which phrase means to get tired? | *Run out of steam | Full steam ahead | Let off steam | Steam train
exam: In the sentence «Heat milk and a skin forms on top, which traps steam», what does the skin do? | It makes the steam invisible forever | It cools the milk down | *It traps the steam until the milk rushes over the edge | It turns the steam into ice
